      "notes": "Original composition; no existing melody, progression, or arrangement was used. This is the five-voice piece: DPCM carries kick/snare while the noise lane remains an independent metallic clock. The custom x-kinetic-breach-osc deliberately has no duty macro, so V00/V01/V02/V03 repaint every section instead of being overwritten next tick. Fractional tempo 160 produces the documented 6,6,5,6,6,5,6,5 row-length cycle. STRUCTURAL METRE: pulse 2 carries a 12-row cell across frames 7-9 with entries 0, 8, 4 (7:0 through 9:52); triangle simultaneously uses a 6+6+4 tresillo bass. Pulse 2 is sparse counterpoint rather than echo: its attacks sit mostly between the lead and its contour opposes the lead at shared phrase peaks. Exactly one metric surprise, D00 at 6:59, strips a beat from the airlock. Drum identity changes on three dimensions: DPCM density, noise subdivision, and metal/open-hat timbre; ghost snare hits use volume 5, and every seam fill is unique. HARMONY: frames 7-10 move Fm-Bb-Eb-Ab, a four-link descending-fifths chain; frame 10 then doubles the harmonic rhythm to two chords per bar. Non-diatonic device one is Db major, the flat-II field at frame 6:0. Device two is E major, a chromatic mediant at frame 10:0 (pulse notes E/G-sharp/B), separated from the airlock. WRITTEN DISSONANCE: Db6 attacks over C at 6:0 and resolves to C6 at 6:8; F6 is held as a ninth over Eb at 8:56 and resolves to Eb6 at 9:0. The final G dominant implication resolves to C at 12:60 before B01. Q32 and R32 are decimal 50 and are each disarmed with 200 before another trigger. Frame 1 restores duty V01, P80, vibrato cancellation and explicit instruments/volumes; all lanes cut or finish before the seam. Preview RMS measured -22.67 dBFS; the declared window is the suite-standard measured value plus or minus 1.5 dB."
